{
  "id": "invert-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "invert-binary-tree",
  "slug": "invert-binary-tree",
  "title": "Invert Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "grind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "inverting a tree means every node's left and right subtree swap - what's the base case for a missing node?",
    "write a helper that inverts the subtree rooted at a given index and writes the result back into the same positions",
    "recursively invert the left subtree and the right subtree first, then swap what ended up at index 2*i+1 and 2*i+2",
    "you can also do this iteratively with a queue or stack, swapping children as you visit each node - either approach works"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "swapping the values at 2*i+1 and 2*i+2 without recursing into the swapped subtrees first, which only inverts one level",
    "forgetting that a missing node (sentinel or out of bounds) still needs to be treated consistently when swapping",
    "building a completely new array with a different indexing scheme instead of preserving the heap-index encoding"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees in these problems are encoded as a flat array named `tree`\nusing complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node here\" - real node values always fall between -100 and 100, so -101 can never be a genuine value\n\nFor example, `[4, 2, 7, 1, 3, 6, 9]` encodes this tree, which inverts\n(left and right children swapped at every node) to `[4, 7, 2, 9, 6, 3, 1]`:\n\n```\n      4                    4\n     / \\                  / \\\n    2   7    invert ->   7   2\n   / \\ / \\              / \\ / \\\n  1  3 6  9            9  6 3  1\n```\n\n**Given a tree encoded this way, invert it (for every node, swap its\nleft and right subtrees) and return the inverted tree using the same\nflat-array encoding.**\n\nTrailing sentinel positions may be trimmed or kept, as long as every\nreal node's children resolve correctly under the encoding rules above.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= number of real nodes <= 100",
      "every real node value is between -100 and 100 inclusive",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty tree, represented as [-101]",
      "a single node with no children",
      "a tree where every node has only a left child (becomes only-right-child after inverting)",
      "a perfectly symmetric tree, which inverts to itself in shape"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
